# STREAM API

## Definition

Stream API is used to process collections in a functional style.

```java
list.stream()
```

---

## Stream Pipeline

```text
Collection
    ↓
stream()
    ↓
filter()
    ↓
map()
    ↓
collect()
```

---

## Important Methods

### filter()

Used to select elements.

```java
.filter(n -> n > 10)
```

Uses: Predicate

---

### map()

Used to transform elements.

```java
.map(n -> n * 2)
```

Uses: Function

---

### forEach()

Used to perform action on each element.

```java
.forEach(System.out::println)
```

Uses: Consumer

---

### collect()

Used to collect result.

```java
.collect(Collectors.toList())
```

Returns List

---

### count()

Returns total elements.

```java
.count()
```

Returns long

---

### findFirst()

Returns first element.

```java
.findFirst()
```

Returns Optional<T>

---

### sorted()

Ascending order.

```java
.sorted()
```

Descending:

```java
.sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
```

---

### distinct()

Removes duplicates.

```java
.distinct()
```

---

### limit()

Returns first N elements.

```java
.limit(3)
```

---

## Intermediate vs Terminal Operations

### Intermediate Operations

Return Stream.

* filter()
* map()
* sorted()
* distinct()
* limit()

### Terminal Operations

End Stream.

* forEach()
* collect()
* count()
* findFirst()

---

## Stream Rules

### Rule 1

One Stream object can be consumed only once.

```java
Stream<Integer> s = list.stream();
s.count();
s.count(); // Error
```

### Rule 2

A Collection can create multiple Streams.

```java
list.stream().count();

list.stream().forEach(System.out::println);
```

Valid.

---

## Lazy Evaluation

Processing starts only after Terminal Operation.

```java
list.stream()
    .filter(n -> n > 10)
    .map(n -> n * 2);
```

Nothing executes.

```java
list.stream()
    .filter(n -> n > 10)
    .map(n -> n * 2)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

Executes.

